ANGLE's platform detection is a list of operating systems with no generic
POSIX arm on the end of it. __linux__ is checked, and after it a list that
names FreeBSD, OpenBSD, NetBSD, DragonFly, Solaris, glibc, the Hurd, QNX,
Fuchsia and Haiku - ten answers to the question "is this a POSIX system"
that the system already answers itself.

__unix__ is that answer. Every operating system in the list but Fuchsia
defines it, so this subsumes most of the list rather than lengthening it,
and it mentions no operating system at all - which is the point: an
out-of-tree POSIX target is exactly the case a list of names cannot cover.

What it costs is nothing on any platform ANGLE ships on, because every one
of them is matched by an earlier arm of the same #if.

Without it src/common/platform.h stops with "#error Unsupported platform."
and takes the 281 objects behind it with it - angle_common, the GLSL
translator, and angle_gpu_info_util, which is where this build gets its
answer to "what GPU does this machine have".

diff --git a/third_party/angle/src/common/platform.h b/third_party/angle/src/common/platform.h
index cae59a1..a047c40 100644
--- a/third_party/angle/src/common/platform.h
+++ b/third_party/angle/src/common/platform.h
@@ -25,7 +25,7 @@
 #    define ANGLE_PLATFORM_POSIX 1
 #elif defined(__FreeBSD__) || defined(__OpenBSD__) || defined(__NetBSD__) ||              \
     defined(__DragonFly__) || defined(__sun) || defined(__GLIBC__) || defined(__GNU__) || \
-    defined(__QNX__) || defined(__Fuchsia__) || defined(__HAIKU__)
+    defined(__QNX__) || defined(__Fuchsia__) || defined(__HAIKU__) || defined(__unix__)
 #    define ANGLE_PLATFORM_POSIX 1
 #else
 #    error Unsupported platform.
